## The missing information is concrete

Today an anchored non-genesis copy contains `entry.at.kind` in the entry's
hashed bytes. Its exact caller anchor therefore binds that value when the
copy matches its fact. Under section 8, a non-genesis entry contains only the
sequence's genesis digest, and its `FactRef.at` contains `{scope,genesis}`.
Neither reveals the kind. A genesis digest is not its seed's preimage.

The missing evidence is the canonical genesis entry whose hash equals
`FactRef.at.genesis`, with a seed whose digest gives `FactRef.at.scope`.
Its verified `input.seed.kind` supplies the required kind. An authorized
exporter can know that kind from its private birth read, but a later
`foreignSources` row carrying the kind would remain descriptive metadata.
It cannot prove the seed-kind binding to an offline reader that has no
birth bytes. A finite reviewed definition pin is an allowlist fact, not a
proof that this particular sequence has that birth.

There is no gap when the actual retained copy is genesis, or when the
needed genesis bytes already exist in the permitted retained set and pass
all identity checks. Those cases can use verified kind without another
fetch or export. The question concerns non-genesis private copies whose
permitted closure omits genesis.

## The existing witness exercises the affected case

`replay/test/archive.test.ts:133–194`, titled “exact caller anchors classify
reviewed private Register copies without admitting unknown archive names”,
builds real Register and Directory judgments in a labelled in-memory fixture.
It creates a Register founding act and confirmed outcome, then uses that
non-genesis outcome as the Directory's creation source. It anchors the
retained Register facts and expects full Replay `consistent`, with the
anchors listed and zero private journal reads. Without anchors it expects
missing dependency. Wrong pin or kind must not take the private shortcut.

That test does not run a live Worker or export private history. Its current
successful expectation must not survive by assigning an unverified kind to
a new verified-genesis context.

## Two possible ruled behaviors

**A. Preserve the portable result with an explicit replay-only trust basis.**
Allow a bounded, closed caller trust record for the exact SequenceRef, kind,
and finite native pin alongside the exact fact anchor. Keep this record
separate from verified birth evidence and show the kind assumption in the
report's trust statement. Archive metadata alone must not manufacture the
caller assumption. Live admission still reads and retains verified genesis;
existing `from.pin` and native birth-proof requirements still require their
real evidence.

This keeps private genesis outside the archive. It adds a new explicit trust
assumption and a small caller/manifest/report interface. It needs a planner
clarification of section 8 for this narrow offline path. Its witnesses must
bind the exact scope, genesis, kind, pin and fact, preserve the finite cohort,
and distinguish a matching caller assertion from tampered or absent archive
metadata. No result may call this verified genesis or Native birth proof.

**B. Require verified genesis and report incomplete when it is absent.**
Use only an already permitted, verified genesis copy to recover kind. If a
kind-dependent new-format judgment lacks that copy, stop with the owning
incomplete or missing-dependency result rather than guess. Do not fetch or
export a private source merely to retain the previous successful result.
A separately authorized caller-supplied genesis can serve only after the
same complete identity and bounds checks; no automatic closure expansion
follows from this option.

This follows the strict sender-kind rule without a new trust assumption,
but the existing non-genesis exact-anchor fixture loses its prior portable
`consistent` result when birth is absent. The planner must accept that
behavioral cost. Strengthen the existing owner to show the truthful failure
with zero private reads, and show success only when verified genesis already
belongs to the allowed input set. The exact result vocabulary belongs to
its actual missing-evidence boundary, not a new successful anchor shortcut.

Both choices preserve copy classification, exact caller anchors, finite
catalog selection, admission and byte-work ceilings, and the ban on broad
private-genesis discovery or export. Neither changes old journals: retained
old-format Replay remains their reader. Other c+d source mapping can
continue while this one construction choice awaits a ruling.
